1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,178
This wall is made of stone.

2
00:00:02,178 --> 00:00:03,340
But look closely.

3
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:04,844
Parts of it are glass.

4
00:00:04,844 --> 00:00:12,500
Black, bubbled, dripping glass, fused onto solid rock, on a Scottish hilltop, two and a half thousand years ago.

5
00:00:12,966 --> 00:00:17,127
To melt rock like this, you need around a thousand degrees.

6
00:00:17,127 --> 00:00:17,832
For hours.

7
00:00:17,832 --> 00:00:23,333
That is far hotter than any campfire, and no ancient writer left instructions.

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:25,956
So the internet has answers.

9
00:00:25,956 --> 00:00:27,496
Lasers from the sky.

10
00:00:27,496 --> 00:00:28,651
Plasma weapons.

11
00:00:28,651 --> 00:00:30,500
An ancient atomic blast.

12
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:37,200
Some say only a lost super-civilisation, or visitors, could melt solid stone like this.

13
00:00:37,666 --> 00:00:40,268
So tonight we do something different.

14
00:00:40,268 --> 00:00:41,604
We follow the heat.

15
00:00:41,604 --> 00:00:49,199
A gauge that starts at cold hilltop air and climbs, one chapter at a time, until the stone itself gives way.

16
00:00:49,666 --> 00:00:50,853
Eight questions.

17
00:00:50,853 --> 00:00:51,744
A scorecard.

18
00:00:51,744 --> 00:00:55,008
And a verdict on Scotland's vitrified forts.

19
00:00:55,008 --> 00:00:56,566
Let's light the fire.

20
00:01:01,033 --> 00:01:01,988
Question one.

21
00:01:01,988 --> 00:01:03,678
Where are these places?

22
00:01:03,678 --> 00:01:06,103
Cold air, twenty degrees at most.

23
00:01:06,103 --> 00:01:14,333
Tap o' Noth in Aberdeenshire rises about five hundred and sixty metres, the second highest hillfort in Scotland.

24
00:01:14,799 --> 00:01:23,409
More than sixty vitrified forts are known in Scotland, from Argyll to Aberdeenshire, from Galloway to Sutherland.

25
00:01:23,409 --> 00:01:29,200
A geologist, John Williams, first described them in seventeen seventy-seven.

26
00:01:29,666 --> 00:01:31,624
They are not only Scottish.

27
00:01:31,624 --> 00:01:37,786
Burned, fused walls turn up in Ireland, France, Germany, Bohemia, Hungary and Sweden.

28
00:01:37,786 --> 00:01:41,266
Whatever this was, it was a habit across Europe.

29
00:01:41,733 --> 00:01:44,978
Up close, it does not look like window glass.

30
00:01:44,978 --> 00:01:51,252
It looks like pale rocks set in a dark, slag-like crust, with bubbles, or frozen drips.

31
00:01:51,252 --> 00:01:53,200
Easy to walk straight past.

32
00:01:53,666 --> 00:02:00,508
At Dun Deardail, high above Glen Nevis, excavation dated the fort to around five hundred B C.

33
00:02:00,508 --> 00:02:06,466
And then something ended it, in what the archaeologists call a catastrophic fire.

34
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:10,469
So the places are real, and the melted rock is real.

35
00:02:10,469 --> 00:02:12,713
But where did the fuel come from?

36
00:02:12,713 --> 00:02:15,433
Next mark on the gauge: the wall itself.

37
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:16,784
Question two.

38
00:02:16,784 --> 00:02:18,552
What was the wall made of?

39
00:02:18,552 --> 00:02:24,674
Three hundred degrees, hotter than any kitchen oven, and so far it is just stone and wood.

40
00:02:24,674 --> 00:02:25,966
Timber-laced stone.

41
00:02:26,433 --> 00:02:28,646
These ramparts were timber-laced.

42
00:02:28,646 --> 00:02:32,267
Beams laid in and across the wall, packed with rubble.

43
00:02:32,267 --> 00:02:37,833
And in every one of these forts, no lime, no cement, no mortar has ever been found.

44
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:50,733
At Craig Phadrig, near Inverness, the inner wall is a heavy vitrified mass spread about thirty feet thick, around an oval summit roughly two hundred and forty by seventy-five feet.

45
00:02:51,199 --> 00:02:52,850
Here is the first clue.

46
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:59,379
The vitrified masses are always heavily undercut, with hollows where something burned away.

47
00:02:59,379 --> 00:03:03,900
That is the shape fire leaves from the inside, along the beams.

48
00:03:04,366 --> 00:03:06,967
Timber is useful to archaeologists.

49
00:03:06,967 --> 00:03:17,666
Charcoal from those beams can be radiocarbon dated, and most dates fall in the first millennium B C, roughly seven hundred to three hundred B C.

50
00:03:18,133 --> 00:03:20,920
And timber lacing was no brief fashion.

51
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:28,566
It was used for well over a thousand years, from the early first millennium B C into the ninth century A D.

52
00:03:29,033 --> 00:03:31,345
So the wall carried its own fuel.

53
00:03:31,345 --> 00:03:35,620
A hill, a stone rampart, and tonnes of wood hidden inside it.

54
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:38,633
Next question: who first noticed the glass?

55
00:03:39,099 --> 00:03:40,173
Question three.

56
00:03:40,173 --> 00:03:41,462
Who noticed first?

57
00:03:41,462 --> 00:03:47,332
In seventeen seventy-seven, the geologist John Williams described the fused ruins.

58
00:03:47,332 --> 00:03:52,199
A debate began that has not stopped for two hundred and fifty years.

59
00:03:52,666 --> 00:04:03,966
By the nineteenth century, antiquarians argued the builders had fused the stone on purpose, to bind the wall into one solid mass, a kind of natural cement.

60
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:07,009
The old encyclopaedia was careful.

61
00:04:07,009 --> 00:04:16,633
The stones might be only partly melted, or cemented at the edges, or coated in glassy enamel, and rarely one solid glassy mass.

62
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:24,782
In nineteen oh six, a lieutenant-colonel tested open fires on selected stones, and published the results.

63
00:04:24,782 --> 00:04:26,684
Fire could fuse some rock.

64
00:04:26,684 --> 00:04:28,733
But how and why stayed open.

65
00:04:29,199 --> 00:04:31,515
Different forts, different rocks.

66
00:04:31,515 --> 00:04:34,673
Red sandstone, conglomerate, diorite, schist.

67
00:04:34,673 --> 00:04:39,866
Builders used what the hill gave them, and many of those rocks were fused.

68
00:04:40,333 --> 00:04:46,300
By the nineteen thirties, someone finally decided to stop arguing and light a bonfire.

69
00:04:46,766 --> 00:04:47,817
Question four.

70
00:04:47,817 --> 00:04:49,618
Can ordinary fire do it?

71
00:04:49,618 --> 00:04:51,269
Eight hundred degrees.

72
00:04:51,269 --> 00:04:58,923
In the nineteen thirties, the archaeologist Vere Gordon Childe excavated the fort at Finavon in Angus.

73
00:04:58,923 --> 00:05:01,100
Then he tried to recreate it.

74
00:05:01,566 --> 00:05:10,833
With Wallace Thorneycroft, he built a test wall about six feet wide and six feet high, laced with horizontal timbers and packed with rubble.

75
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:14,100
They lit brushwood fires against the face.

76
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:15,366
The timbers caught.

77
00:05:15,366 --> 00:05:19,566
The wall burned hard, and after about three hours it collapsed.

78
00:05:20,033 --> 00:05:28,406
Digging into it, they found the core of the rubble had been red hot, somewhere around eight hundred to twelve hundred degrees.

79
00:05:28,406 --> 00:05:30,600
At the bottom lay vitrified rock.

80
00:05:31,066 --> 00:05:32,863
Droplets of melted rock.

81
00:05:32,863 --> 00:05:34,959
Casts of the burned timbers.

82
00:05:34,959 --> 00:05:39,825
The largest piece weighed about one hundred and eighty kilograms.

83
00:05:39,825 --> 00:05:44,766
A timber-laced wall, plus ordinary fire, had made vitrified stone.

84
00:05:45,233 --> 00:05:47,779
The experiment taught a subtle lesson.

85
00:05:47,779 --> 00:05:49,454
Wood alone is not enough.

86
00:05:49,454 --> 00:05:53,609
The timber first has to turn to charcoal, which soaks up heat.

87
00:05:53,609 --> 00:05:58,366
Only then does glowing charcoal, with air moving through it, melt rock.

88
00:05:58,833 --> 00:06:00,207
So fire can do it.

89
00:06:00,207 --> 00:06:04,023
But a test wall is small, and real forts are huge.

90
00:06:04,023 --> 00:06:07,000
Next on the gauge: the television fire.

91
00:06:07,466 --> 00:06:08,473
Question five.

92
00:06:08,473 --> 00:06:10,559
Can fire do it at full scale?

93
00:06:10,559 --> 00:06:12,069
One thousand degrees.

94
00:06:12,069 --> 00:06:17,463
In nineteen eighty, Yorkshire Television set out to find out, for Arthur C.

95
00:06:17,463 --> 00:06:19,333
Clarke's Mysterious World.

96
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:31,800
Archaeologist Ian Ralston supervised a full-scale pine-laced wall, built in March and April, at the edge of a waste disposal tip on Tullos Hill in Aberdeen.

97
00:06:32,266 --> 00:06:34,952
They lit it in less than ideal conditions.

98
00:06:34,952 --> 00:06:38,278
Twenty-eight hours later, the wall was still alight.

99
00:06:38,278 --> 00:06:40,899
It had to be pulled down, to be searched.

100
00:06:41,366 --> 00:06:44,766
The result was limited, local vitrification.

101
00:06:44,766 --> 00:06:47,625
About three kilograms of glassy rock.

102
00:06:47,625 --> 00:06:48,707
Not a rampart.

103
00:06:48,707 --> 00:06:49,866
Not even close.

104
00:06:50,333 --> 00:06:56,566
On screen, Ralston also piled stones on Tap o' Noth and lit a massive bonfire.

105
00:06:56,566 --> 00:06:58,964
A few partly vitrified stones.

106
00:06:58,964 --> 00:07:03,199
For large-scale forts, the experiment gave no answer.

107
00:07:03,666 --> 00:07:06,701
To fans of the mystery, that was the opening.

108
00:07:06,701 --> 00:07:11,691
If a professional test could not do it, then maybe something stranger did.

109
00:07:11,691 --> 00:07:15,333
This is where the lasers and the atomic blast come in.

110
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:19,097
But a failed test does not prove impossible.

111
00:07:19,097 --> 00:07:22,019
Fuel, wind, and wall design all matter.

112
00:07:22,019 --> 00:07:26,066
So how hot, and for how long, did the real fires burn?

113
00:07:26,533 --> 00:07:27,501
Question six.

114
00:07:27,501 --> 00:07:28,767
How hot, exactly?

115
00:07:28,767 --> 00:07:30,480
Eleven hundred degrees.

116
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:40,833
Analysis of the glass in vitrified forts suggests temperatures averaging between nine hundred and eleven hundred and fifty degrees Celsius.

117
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:46,560
Researchers heated rock from Wincobank, a vitrified hillfort in Sheffield.

118
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:54,166
The match was between one thousand and fifty and twelve hundred and fifty degrees, for more than ten hours.

119
00:07:54,633 --> 00:07:55,334
Ten hours.

120
00:07:55,334 --> 00:07:58,838
And the glass often runs right around the circuit.

121
00:07:58,838 --> 00:08:05,566
The researchers thought that makes an accident, or a brief battle fire, unlikely for many forts.

122
00:08:06,033 --> 00:08:10,661
But ten hours at eleven hundred degrees is not alien technology.

123
00:08:10,661 --> 00:08:15,000
An Iron Age smith's furnace reached around thirteen hundred.

124
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,833
People had been melting metal for thousands of years.

125
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:23,622
The heat is volcano heat, which is why we call it vitrified.

126
00:08:23,622 --> 00:08:32,266
Forestry and Land Scotland describe it simply: past a thousand degrees, the rubble melts, the rocks fracture, then flow.

127
00:08:32,733 --> 00:08:35,504
So the heat was within Iron Age reach.

128
00:08:35,504 --> 00:08:37,474
What is left is the motive.

129
00:08:37,474 --> 00:08:39,224
Who sets fire to a wall?

130
00:08:39,224 --> 00:08:41,266
And why do it for ten hours?

131
00:08:41,733 --> 00:08:42,820
Question seven.

132
00:08:42,820 --> 00:08:43,980
Why burn a wall?

133
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:46,010
Theory one: an enemy did it.

134
00:08:46,010 --> 00:08:50,723
Conquerors torch the timber, the fort falls, and the stone melts.

135
00:08:50,723 --> 00:08:53,333
It is the oldest explanation of all.

136
00:08:53,799 --> 00:09:03,342
The problem: fires like that need to be tended for hours, and vitrification did not breach the walls, nor make them easier to climb.

137
00:09:03,342 --> 00:09:05,366
A battle fire is a poor fit.

138
00:09:05,833 --> 00:09:11,766
Theory two: the people burned it themselves, at the end of its life, as a ritual closure.

139
00:09:11,766 --> 00:09:17,899
The current expert consensus favours deliberate destruction, by victors or by the occupants.

140
00:09:18,366 --> 00:09:22,407
Theory three: the builders melted the wall to strengthen it.

141
00:09:22,407 --> 00:09:23,956
Heat cracks big blocks.

142
00:09:23,956 --> 00:09:31,500
But Wadsworth and Hearne found the fine rubble between them fuses, and gets stronger, welding the wall together.

143
00:09:31,966 --> 00:09:43,978
At Broborg in Sweden, built between four hundred and five hundred and fifty A D, a twenty twenty-two study concluded the vitrification was done to construct the fort.

144
00:09:43,978 --> 00:09:46,366
A skill lost once mortar arrived.

145
00:09:46,833 --> 00:09:48,170
The dates scatter.

146
00:09:48,170 --> 00:09:51,216
Many are Iron Age, others early medieval.

147
00:09:51,216 --> 00:09:56,193
Clatchard Craig in Fife burned in the sixth or seventh century A D.

148
00:09:56,193 --> 00:09:59,833
There was no single moment, and no single reason.

149
00:10:00,299 --> 00:10:02,149
One idea is gaining ground.

150
00:10:02,149 --> 00:10:09,000
Some of these may not have been forts at all, but monuments, built to be burned, as a show of power.

151
00:10:09,466 --> 00:10:10,617
Question eight.

152
00:10:10,617 --> 00:10:12,458
The extraordinary claim.

153
00:10:12,458 --> 00:10:15,756
Lasers, plasma, or an ancient atomic blast.

154
00:10:15,756 --> 00:10:20,359
Forts melted from above, in a flash, by a lost super-weapon.

155
00:10:20,359 --> 00:10:21,433
Let's test it.

156
00:10:21,899 --> 00:10:23,463
Test one: temperature.

157
00:10:23,463 --> 00:10:26,874
A nuclear explosion reaches millions of degrees.

158
00:10:26,874 --> 00:10:30,783
The forts needed around eleven hundred, cooking slowly.

159
00:10:30,783 --> 00:10:32,133
Wrong kind of heat.

160
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:34,151
Test two: the pattern.

161
00:10:34,151 --> 00:10:36,478
A flash would strike the surface.

162
00:10:36,478 --> 00:10:42,190
These masses are fused inside the wall, undercut, with casts of the burned beams.

163
00:10:42,190 --> 00:10:44,799
The fire came from within the timber.

164
00:10:45,266 --> 00:10:46,911
Test three: the trail.

165
00:10:46,911 --> 00:10:53,191
The nineteen forty-five Trinity test left green glass and clear chemical signatures.

166
00:10:53,191 --> 00:10:58,200
Nobody has reported finding anything like that at a vitrified fort.

167
00:10:58,666 --> 00:11:00,588
Test four: the patchwork.

168
00:11:00,588 --> 00:11:05,047
Vitrification is uneven, section by section, fort by fort.

169
00:11:05,047 --> 00:11:09,045
Weapons do not melt one stretch and ignore the next.

170
00:11:09,045 --> 00:11:11,966
A fire fed by beams does exactly that.

171
00:11:12,433 --> 00:11:14,856
Another suggestion is Greek fire.

172
00:11:14,856 --> 00:11:21,684
But it was most likely petroleum, hard to carry that far, and not hot or long-lasting enough.

173
00:11:21,684 --> 00:11:23,299
Local wood was easier.

174
00:11:23,766 --> 00:11:25,725
And no weapon is needed.

175
00:11:25,725 --> 00:11:28,745
Childe's bonfire made vitrified rock.

176
00:11:28,745 --> 00:11:31,765
A lab furnace reproduced the melting.

177
00:11:31,765 --> 00:11:34,296
Timber, charcoal, air and time.

178
00:11:34,296 --> 00:11:36,499
The technology is ordinary.

179
00:11:36,966 --> 00:11:39,738
What remains unknown is not the tool.

180
00:11:39,738 --> 00:11:41,012
It is the reason.

181
00:11:41,012 --> 00:11:43,184
That is not an alien mystery.

182
00:11:43,184 --> 00:11:44,533
It is a human one.

183
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:45,765
The verdict.

184
00:11:45,765 --> 00:11:47,933
Time to score the vitrified forts.

185
00:11:48,399 --> 00:11:51,780
Fused rock in real Scottish walls: evidence.

186
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:56,158
Timber-laced ramparts with burned beam hollows: evidence.

187
00:11:56,158 --> 00:11:59,538
Eleven hundred degrees, for hours: evidence.

188
00:11:59,538 --> 00:12:03,533
Reproduced by fire, partly, at small scale: partial.

189
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:09,008
Why they were burned: debated, a ritual, a victory, or a method.

190
00:12:09,008 --> 00:12:12,451
Ancient atomic blast or lasers: no evidence.

191
00:12:12,451 --> 00:12:15,033
Greek fire or visitors: unlikely.

192
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:19,914
Case closed, and the walls still burn in the imagination.

193
00:12:19,914 --> 00:12:21,075
Ancient lasers?

194
00:12:21,075 --> 00:12:21,307
No.

195
00:12:21,307 --> 00:12:27,890
An ancient timber fire hot enough to melt rock, set for reasons we still argue about?

196
00:12:27,890 --> 00:12:28,200
Yes.

197
00:12:28,666 --> 00:12:33,784
Somewhere, twenty-five centuries ago, a whole hilltop burned through the night.

198
00:12:33,784 --> 00:12:36,311
They knew exactly what they were doing.

199
00:12:36,311 --> 00:12:37,866
We just do not know why.

200
00:12:38,333 --> 00:12:43,964
Next case: a Pakistani city, said to be destroyed by an ancient nuclear blast.

201
00:12:43,964 --> 00:12:50,533
Skeletons in the street, glassy rocks, and a story that began in a nineteen seventies book.

202
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,333
Lasers or burning ramparts, which side are you on?

203
00:12:54,333 --> 00:12:59,933
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
